    Section(
      title: '+ Discuss the workup for recurrent stroke in this patient, including cardiac source evaluation and secondary prevention strategy.',
      content:
          '**Etiology of Sequential Bilateral Strokes**:\n\nThis patient\'s bilateral MCA strokes are most likely **cardioembolic** in origin based on:\n1. **Left atrial appendage thrombus** identified on echocardiography\n2. **New-onset atrial fibrillation** detected between the two events\n3. **Subtherapeutic anticoagulation** (INR 1.3 on heparin bridge) at the time of the second stroke\n4. **MCA territory distribution** -- large vessel occlusions typical of cardiac emboli\n\n**Comprehensive Stroke Workup -- Confirming and Expanding**:\n\n**Cardiac Evaluation**:\n- **Transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE)**: Superior to TTE for visualizing left atrial appendage thrombus, atrial septal abnormalities, and aortic arch atheroma; confirm thrombus resolution on anticoagulation\n- **Extended cardiac monitoring**: If not already on continuous telemetry, ensure at least 30-day cardiac monitoring to quantify atrial fibrillation burden and assess rate control\n- **CHA2DS2-VASc Score**: Age 70 (1), hypertension (1), diabetes (1), vascular disease (1), prior stroke/TIA (2) = **6** -- very high risk; lifetime anticoagulation mandatory\n- **HAS-BLED Score**: Assess bleeding risk to guide anticoagulation choice\n\n**Vascular Imaging**:\n- CT or MR angiography of head and neck (if not already completed): Assess for concurrent large vessel atherosclerosis, carotid stenosis, or intracranial stenosis as contributing factors\n- Assess bilateral MCA patency status post-intervention\n\n**Laboratory**:\n- Hypercoagulability panel: Consider antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, beta-2 glycoprotein) -- particularly in recurrent stroke\n- A1c optimization (currently 9.2% -- significant contributor to vascular risk)\n- Lipid panel (ensure LDL < 70 mg/dL on high-intensity statin)\n\n**Secondary Prevention Strategy**:\n1. **Anticoagulation**: Apixaban 5 mg BID (already initiated) -- preferred DOAC for stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation; superior to warfarin for this indication based on ARISTOTLE trial data; no need for concurrent antiplatelet therapy (increases bleeding without benefit)\n2. **Rate/rhythm control**: Metoprolol for rate control; cardiology consultation for rhythm control strategy (cardioversion vs. ablation if patient recovers sufficiently)\n3. **Left atrial appendage closure**: Consider Watchman device if anticoagulation becomes contraindicated (e.g., falls, hemorrhagic conversion)\n4. **Aggressive risk factor management**: Blood pressure goal <130/80; A1c goal <7%; high-intensity statin; smoking cessation maintained; weight management\n5. **Repeat imaging**: MRI brain at 3-6 months to assess for hemorrhagic transformation on anticoagulation',
