      content:
          '**Prognosis in Bilateral MCA Strokes**:\n\n**Cognitive Recovery**:\n- Bilateral MCA territory infarctions carry a **very poor prognosis** for meaningful cognitive recovery\n- The combination of left hemisphere damage (language networks) and right hemisphere damage (attention/visuospatial networks) results in profound cognitive impairment that typically does not recover to a functional level\n- The left MCA stroke caused global aphasia; the right MCA stroke added left neglect and further attentional deficits -- together these eliminate most avenues for cognitive rehabilitation\n- MMSE of 4/30 at 3-5 weeks post-stroke predicts very limited recovery; most studies show patients with initial MMSE <10 after bilateral strokes rarely improve to functional independence\n\n**Motor Recovery**:\n- Bilateral hemiparesis (quadriparesis) eliminates the typical compensation strategy of using the unaffected side\n- The right side (from the earlier left MCA stroke) has had 5 weeks and shows only trace to poor voluntary movement with developing spasticity\n- The left side (from the more recent right MCA stroke) is flaccid and shows minimal voluntary movement\n- Functional ambulation is extremely unlikely; ability to participate in transfers is uncertain\n\n**Dysphagia**:\n- Bilateral hemispheric strokes cause severe oropharyngeal dysphagia\n- Failed VFSS with aspiration on all consistencies -- PEG tube dependence is likely permanent\n- May trial repeat swallow evaluation at 3-6 months, but bilateral cortical swallowing center damage has poor recovery trajectory\n\n**Overall Functional Prognosis**:\n- Most likely outcome: long-term total dependence for all ADLs and mobility\n- High risk for medical complications: aspiration pneumonia, DVT/PE, pressure injuries, UTIs, contractures\n- Mortality rate for bilateral large MCA strokes is high -- approximately 40-60% at 1 year in published series\n- Those who survive typically require 24-hour skilled nursing care\n\n**Prognostic Indicators to Follow**:\n- Serial cognitive assessments every 2-4 weeks to detect any trajectory of improvement\n- Emergence of consistent command-following or purposeful communication would significantly change prognosis\n- Return of motor function, particularly in the right upper extremity (longer time since injury)\n- Development of reliable yes/no communication (even with eye blinks) would be a positive indicator',
