    Section(
      title: '+ How do you approach cognitive rehabilitation and functional goal-setting in a patient with severe bilateral hemispheric damage?',
      content:
          '**Rehabilitation Approach for Severe Bilateral Stroke**:\n\n**Justification for Acute Rehabilitation Admission**:\n- Patient must demonstrate ability to tolerate 3 hours of therapy per day (can be divided among disciplines)\n- Even in severe impairment, therapy can focus on: positioning, family training, preventing complications, and assessing recovery trajectory\n- If patient cannot tolerate or participate, consider subacute rehabilitation (SNF) with plan to reassess\n\n**Cognitive Stimulation Program**:\n- **Sensory stimulation protocol**: Structured multisensory stimulation (visual, auditory, tactile, olfactory) to promote arousal and awareness\n- **Environmental enrichment**: Familiar voices (family recordings), music therapy, photographs, favorite TV/radio programs\n- **Coma recovery scale assessment**: Use the JFK Coma Recovery Scale-Revised (CRS-R) serially to track emergence from minimally conscious state vs. vegetative state\n- **Monitor for improvement**: Any consistent command-following, visual tracking, or purposeful vocalization would indicate meaningful cognitive recovery potential\n\n**Functional Goals (Realistic for Current Level)**:\n1. **Positioning program**: Turning schedule Q2H for pressure injury prevention and healing; proper bed positioning to prevent contractures; upright positioning in tilt-in-space wheelchair for 2-4 hours daily to improve arousal and respiratory function\n2. **ROM and contracture prevention**: Daily passive ROM to all extremities; resting hand splints bilaterally; ankle positioning devices to prevent equinus\n3. **Pulmonary hygiene**: Aggressive suctioning protocol; chest physiotherapy; upright positioning to reduce aspiration risk from oral secretions\n4. **Family training**: Educate family on positioning, skin checks, ROM exercises, signs of complications (DVT, UTI, AD equivalent, aspiration pneumonia)\n5. **Skin management**: Offload sacral pressure injury; heel suspension boots; nutritional optimization for wound healing (adequate protein via tube feeds)\n6. **PEG tube management**: Ensure adequate nutrition (25-30 kcal/kg, 1.2-1.5 g protein/kg); head of bed elevated 30-45 degrees during and after feeds\n\n**Goals of Care Discussion**:\n- Initiate a family meeting within the first week of rehabilitation admission\n- Present realistic prognosis with compassion but honesty\n- Discuss what constitutes meaningful recovery vs. survival with severe disability\n- Explore the patient\'s previously expressed wishes (advance directives, conversations with family)\n- Address code status, feeding tube continuation, and what level of medical intervention the family desires\n- Involve palliative care consultation to help facilitate these discussions\n- Set a time-limited trial: reassess at 2-4 weeks; if no measurable improvement on CRS-R or functional measures, revisit goals and consider transition to comfort-focused care or long-term care facility',
