    Section(
      title: '+ Describe the process for determining decision-making capacity, surrogate decision-making hierarchy, and guardianship/conservatorship when family members disagree.',
      content:
          '**Decision-Making Capacity Assessment**:\n\n**Capacity vs. Competency**:\n- **Capacity** is a clinical determination made by physicians\n- **Competency** is a legal determination made by courts\n- This patient clearly lacks decision-making capacity: he cannot understand information, appreciate his situation, reason about options, or communicate a choice (all four Appelbaum criteria are absent)\n\n**Surrogate Decision-Making**:\n\n**Hierarchy (varies by state but generally)**:\n1. Health care proxy/durable power of attorney for health care (if designated) -- this patient has none\n2. Legal guardian (if appointed by court)\n3. Spouse\n4. Adult children\n5. Parents\n6. Siblings\n7. Other close relatives or friends\n\n**In this case**: The wife is the default surrogate decision-maker as the spouse, in most state hierarchies. However, the adult children are challenging her decisions.\n\n**Standards for Surrogate Decision-Making**:\n1. **Substituted judgment standard** (preferred): What would the patient have wanted? Based on prior conversations, values, religious beliefs, lifestyle choices. The surrogate should attempt to make the decision the patient would have made.\n2. **Best interest standard** (fallback): When the patient\'s wishes are truly unknown, the surrogate should choose what a reasonable person would consider to be in the patient\'s best interest, weighing benefits and burdens.\n\n**When Family Disagrees -- Conflict Resolution**:\n\n1. **Family meeting with interdisciplinary team**: Include attending physician, social worker, chaplain, nursing, and all involved family members. Explore the root of disagreement -- often it is grief, guilt, misunderstanding of prognosis, or differing values.\n2. **Ethics committee consultation**: If the family meeting does not resolve the conflict, involve the hospital ethics committee for a formal consultation. The ethics committee can mediate, clarify ethical principles, and provide recommendations.\n3. **Palliative care consultation**: Palliative care specialists are trained in facilitating goals-of-care conversations and can help bridge differences.\n4. **Time-limited trial**: Propose a defined period (e.g., 4 weeks) of continued treatment with specific milestones. If milestones are not met, reconvene to reassess. This gives all family members time to process and observe the trajectory.\n5. **Guardianship/Conservatorship (last resort)**:\n   - If the family cannot reach consensus and the conflict is irreconcilable, any party can petition the court for guardianship\n   - The court appoints a guardian with legal authority to make health care decisions\n   - Process involves: petition filing, medical evaluation, court hearing, guardian appointment\n   - Can take weeks to months; emergency guardianship available for urgent decisions\n   - Court may appoint a family member or an independent guardian (attorney or professional guardian)\n\n**Long-Term Care Placement**:\n- If patient remains severely impaired after rehabilitation trial, discharge options include:\n  - Skilled nursing facility with long-term care\n  - Veterans\' home (if applicable)\n  - Home with 24-hour caregiver support (extremely costly; typically not sustainable)\n- Medicaid spend-down may be necessary for long-term nursing facility coverage\n- Social work should begin discharge planning early given the likely prolonged need for placement',
