    Section(
      title: '+ Demonstrate how you would facilitate this family meeting, address the conflict between the wife and daughter, and guide the family toward a unified decision.',
      content:
          '**Model Response -- Facilitating a High-Conflict Family Meeting**:\n\n*[First, allow a moment of silence. Acknowledge the pain in the room.]*\n\n"I can see how much all of you love [patient\'s name], and I can see how painful this is for every one of you. Before we go any further, I want you to know that there are no wrong feelings here. You are all coming from a place of love, even if you see the path forward differently.\n\nMrs. [name], I hear you. Your hope and your faith are powerful, and I respect them deeply. And [daughter\'s name], I hear you too. It sounds like your father shared something very personal with you about his wishes, and you are trying to honor that. Both of those things matter.\n\nBefore we discuss what to do, I want to make sure everyone has the same understanding of where [patient\'s name] is medically. May I share what we know?"\n\n*[Present the medical facts with compassion but clarity]*\n\n"Your husband and father has had two very large strokes affecting both sides of his brain. Right now, he is not able to speak, follow commands, eat, or move his arms or legs in a meaningful way. His brain injury is severe.\n\nI want to be honest with you because I believe you deserve honesty: in my medical experience, and based on the research we have, the chances of him recovering to a point where he could communicate, recognize you, or participate in his own care are very small. I cannot say it is impossible -- and I would never take away your hope -- but I want you to have realistic expectations so you can make decisions that truly reflect what he would want.\n\nNow, [daughter\'s name], you mentioned that your father told you he would not want to live if he could not take care of himself. Can you tell us more about that conversation? When was it, and what specifically did he say?"\n\n*[Allow the daughter to share; then turn to the wife]*\n\n"Mrs. [name], did your husband ever talk to you about what he would want in a situation like this? Even indirectly -- maybe about a friend or family member who was very ill?"\n\n*[Listen to the wife\'s response; validate both perspectives]*\n\n"Here is what I want to suggest. I do not think we need to make every decision today. What I would propose is this: we continue rehabilitation for the next four weeks. During that time, we will do everything we can to give him the best chance of showing us improvement. We will measure his progress using specific tools.\n\nAt the end of four weeks, we will meet again. If he has shown meaningful improvement -- if he is starting to follow commands, communicate, or participate in his care -- we will continue the current path. If he has not shown improvement, I would ask that we revisit this conversation and consider whether continuing aggressive treatment is truly what he would want, or whether we should focus on keeping him comfortable and dignified.\n\nIn the meantime, I do not think we need to change the code status today. But I would like to involve our palliative care team -- and I want to be clear, palliative care is not the same as giving up. Their job is to help manage his comfort and to help your family navigate these decisions together.\n\nI also want to acknowledge something directly: this disagreement between family members is incredibly common and does not mean anyone is wrong or selfish. You are each processing grief differently, and you are each trying to honor a man you love. Our social worker and chaplain are available to meet with any or all of you individually if that would help.\n\nFor you two --" *[addressing the silent adult children]* "-- I want to make sure your voices are heard too. Do you have any thoughts or questions you would like to share?"\n\n**Key Communication Principles Demonstrated**:\n1. **Validated both sides** without taking sides or undermining either position\n2. **Presented medical reality** with compassion but did not sugarcoat prognosis\n3. **Explored the patient\'s values** by asking both the wife and daughter for specific recollections\n4. **Proposed a time-limited trial** as a bridge -- allows the wife to maintain hope while setting objective criteria for reassessment\n5. **Included the silent family members** -- they may hold crucial information or become future decision-makers\n6. **Named the conflict** directly without judgment ("this disagreement is incredibly common")\n7. **Offered resources** (palliative care, social work, chaplaincy) without forcing them\n8. **Did not demand an immediate code status decision** -- allowed time and trust to build\n9. **Separated palliative care from "giving up"** -- a common misconception that must be addressed\n10. **Maintained the physician role as guide and mediator** rather than unilateral decision-maker',
